@*
    A marker element that exists only once this subtree is rendering interactively —
    that is, once the Blazor circuit is attached and @onclick handlers are live.

    Why it exists. Blazor Server prerenders an interactive page as static HTML, so
    every button is in the DOM, visible and enabled, for a beat before it is wired to
    a circuit. A click sent inside that window is dropped silently: the element was
    perfectly clickable, there was simply nothing on the other end yet. Playwright's
    auto-waiting does not save you here, because it waits for the element to be
    actionable, not for the application behind it to be listening. That gap is the
    single largest source of flake in a Blazor Server end-to-end suite, and the usual
    workaround — sleeping for a guessed number of milliseconds — is both slow and
    still wrong on a cold start, where the first circuit takes seconds rather than
    hundreds of milliseconds.

    RendererInfo.IsInteractive is false throughout the prerender pass and true from
    the first interactive render onwards, so the presence of this element is exactly
    the signal the tests need — no JavaScript, no framework internals, no polling on
    a private API that a future release can rename.

    Statically rendered pages (the auth pages, which opt out of interactive routing
    so their forms post as real requests) never render it. That is correct rather
    than a gap: there is no circuit there to wait for.

    See E2ETest.GotoAsync in Fixlosophy.Tests.E2E for the consuming side.
*@
@if (RendererInfo.IsInteractive)
{
    <div id="blazor-ready" hidden data-nosnippet></div>
}
